'''6.''' ''pl.'' Words, language, or expressions in general.
{{smaller block|<poem>Winds! on your wings to heaven her ''accents'' bear,
Such words as heaven alone is fit to hear.</poem>
{{right|''Dryden'', Virgil’s Eclogues, iii.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Deep on their souls the mighty ''accents'' fall,
Like lead that pierces through the walls of clay.</poem>
{{right|''Jones Very'', Poems, p. 77.}}}}
'''7.''' In ''eccles. chanting'', one of the seven forms of modulation used in parts sung by the officiating priest or his assistants, viz., the ''immutable'', ''medium'', ''grave'', ''acute'', ''moderate'', ''interrogative'', ''final''.—'''8.''' In ''music:'' (''a'') A stress or emphasis given to certain notes or parts of bars in a composition. {{smaller|It is divided into two kinds, ''grammatical'' and ''rhetorical'' or ''esthetic''. The first is perfectly regular in its occurrence, always falling on the first part of a bar; the esthetic accent is irregular, and depends on taste and feeling.}} (''b'') A mark placed after the letter representing a note to indicate the octave in which it is found. {{smaller|Thus, if C is in the great octave (see ''octave''), c is an octave above, c′ an octave above that, c″ in the next, and so on.}}
